WEBVTT

NOTE Simplified English, written by hand from the Official Report by an AI assistant (Claude) in the developer's editor, not by the pipeline; not checked by a person. Timing: the verbatim track's cues, held for reading time where the gap allows.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.853
The Presiding Officer: Now, party
leaders question the First Minister.

00:00:11.853 --> 00:00:14.100
First, we welcome a guest:

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:20.720
Her Excellency Sanja Štiglic,
Slovenia's ambassador.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.120
[Applause.]

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.320
Malcolm Offord: John Swinney
became First Minister in May 2024.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.000
He promised a Government
focused on delivery and unity,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.820
with four priorities: child poverty, the
economy, the NHS and public services.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.280
So why did he go to Cardiff for
a summit paid for by taxpayers?

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.500
Why has he chosen distraction and
division instead of delivery and unity?

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.220
Did his day trip to Wales help
any of the four priorities?

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:26.360
The First Minister: I have
led the Government to focus on

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.960
four main themes:

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.875
ending child poverty, growing the economy,

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:38.720
reaching net zero and
better public services.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.980
We are making good progress on that.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.900
That may partly explain why
I won the election on 7 May.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.180
That is an important point.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.280
During the election, I
spoke to people about

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.660
self-determination and Scotland
as an independent country.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.553
With full independence,

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:14.280
we could reach our goals: a stronger
economy and less child poverty.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.380
That is why I went to Cardiff on Monday.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:30.690
Malcolm Offord: Let me explain why John
Swinney is wrong to focus on that now.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:36.730
The last two Westminster and Holyrood
elections were the SNP's worst since 2014.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.030
Under John Swinney, support for
separatist parties is at an all-time low:

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:49.550
1 million votes in May 2016, down from
1.6 million at the 2014 referendum.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.150
[Interruption.]

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.070
The Presiding Officer:
Excuse me, Mr Flynn.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:03:00.470
Malcolm Offord: Mr Flynn makes
more gas than a CalMac ferry.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:09.390
By contrast, 2 million Scots voted to
stay in the UK — for a generation.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:15.375
This so-called Celtic alliance
is just smoke and mirrors,

00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:17.370
distraction and division.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.890
The First Minister must
focus on the day job:

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.550
our weak economy and
falling education standards,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.950
the struggling NHS, broken transport,

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.030
chaotic immigration and
rising welfare costs.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.178
The First Minister:

00:03:38.178 --> 00:03:43.070
The Government's programme covers
every key area of domestic policy.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:47.570
As I told Malcolm Offord,

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:53.770
we are making real progress on all
those issues — which may be why we won.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:59.390
58 SNP MSPs were elected,
and only 17 from Reform.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.550
We won with the biggest gap over our

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.190
nearest rival in the
history of devolution.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:13.950
I need no lectures from a losing force
like Malcolm Offord, the leader of Reform.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.670
On the question of "a generation":

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.101
by the end of the decade,

00:04:21.101 --> 00:04:27.390
a million people in Scotland will
never have voted on independence.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.638
That is one reason why people
here must get the chance

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:36.150
to decide their own future
and choose independence.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.923
Malcolm Offord: After that answer,

00:04:47.923 --> 00:04:51.630
Piddington has more chance of independence
than John Swinney's Scotland.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.450
Why? Because the SNP
has not prepared for it.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:05:01.310
The SNP has had 12 years to make Scotland
the most successful part of the UK.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.223
It could have answered the four
big questions about separation:

00:05:06.223 --> 00:05:08.310
currency, pensions, defence and borders.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.555
Instead, what do we have?

00:05:10.555 --> 00:05:15.550
An economy with no growth. Our
world-famous education trashed.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.550
The NHS on its knees.
Roads and ferries in chaos.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.830
Uncontrolled immigration
upsetting communities.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.510
Police starved of cash. Councils
about to be cut to the bone.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:30.370
Welfare and taxes both at record highs.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:36.130
And a £5 billion black hole, despite
a record £26 billion block grant.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.190
That is chronic mismanagement
of public money —

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.463
and that is without the
missing ScotWind money.

00:05:44.463 --> 00:05:48.690
All of those failures
are in devolved areas.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:56.970
John Swinney cannot blame Westminster for
any of it. This mess was made by the SNP.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:02.830
How can he call Westminster broken
when Holyrood is in such a mess?

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:10.610
Is the real reason John
Swinney went to Cardiff

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:16.230
that he prefers the media to admitting
he is failing his four priorities?

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.810
Scotland does not want a referendum soon —

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:21.970
[Interruption.]

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.786
— so will the First Minister respect

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:32.290
Scotland's wish and leave
it for a generation?

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:38.123
The First Minister: It is
remarkable that Malcolm Offord —

00:06:38.123 --> 00:06:44.440
who lost the election — thinks he
knows what the people of Scotland want

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:51.261
better than I do — and I won the election.

00:06:51.261 --> 00:06:54.580
He seems very agitated
about Scottish independence.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.120
That suggests he fears what we could do

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.140
if we took our own
decisions on the economy:

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:10.020
use Scotland's energy wealth
to lower people's bills,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:16.720
and repair our trade links with Europe,
which Malcolm Offord and his kind wrecked.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:24.700
That may be why more and more people
in Scotland, as recent polls show,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.760
support independence and want that choice.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:34.800
I will make sure Scotland gets that
choice in this parliamentary session.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:47.516
Jackie Baillie: Before my
questions, I offer my condolences,

00:07:47.516 --> 00:07:52.730
and Scottish Labour's, on the
death of former MSP Cathie Craigie.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.730
Cathie was a much-loved colleague.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.110
She was a woman of great determination.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:03.450
The first woman to pass a member's bill
here, protecting mortgage holders.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:08.810
A miner's daughter, she fought
hard for miners' welfare.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.410
She was committed to Labour,

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:15.650
to Parliament and to her constituents
in Cumbernauld and Kilsyth.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.490
Our thoughts are with her family.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:23.890
[Applause.]

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.470
Presiding Officer, it is another
week, another court case.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:34.409
The Scottish Information Commissioner
holds the Government to account.

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:38.250
He is taking the SNP to court

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.650
for refusing to release vital information.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.770
He says the SNP Government risks

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.410
undermining the public's
right to information

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:51.570
and would leave him with
only a "supervisory role".

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:56.057
The SNP Government is
accused of wanting the

00:08:56.057 --> 00:09:00.790
power to decide what the
public can and cannot see.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:06.550
The public's right to know is at
stake. I ask the First Minister:

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:12.890
what matters more — Scots' right to
know what their Government is doing,

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:18.370
or shielding himself, his former
leader and his party from scrutiny?

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:29.350
The First Minister: Before I
answer Jackie Baillie's question,

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:38.710
I fully agree with her words about Cathie
Craigie. She was a very distinguished MSP.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:46.870
She was generous and kind, and full of fun
and good humour. We enjoyed her company.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.978
I join Jackie Baillie in
paying tribute to her,

00:09:50.978 --> 00:09:54.630
and I send my condolences to her family.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.350
On the substance of Jackie
Baillie's question,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:07.050
I can say little: there is a live court
case, and the Government is part of it.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:14.310
Jackie Baillie asked what I think is
important. It is the rule of law.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:24.170
The Government believes its decisions
fully follow the rule of law

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:34.270
on freedom of information, and on
information that court orders restrict.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:39.038
I would be failing in my
duty as First Minister

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:43.330
if the Government did
not act within the law.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:46.130
That is the basis of the
Government's judgment,

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:51.050
and it will be tested in court.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:11:02.030
Jackie Baillie: Thank you. But I have
watched John Swinney for 27 years,

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:06.980
going from "honest John" to
the John Swinney we see now:

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.030
the architect of secret Scotland.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:13.710
This is the man who
deleted WhatsApp messages

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.830
about life-and-death
decisions in the pandemic.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:24.610
This is the man who spends public money
in court to deny people the truth.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:33.350
This is the man who appointed Peter
Murrell, now a convicted fraudster,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.150
and who oversaw a culture of cover-up.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.830
These are not isolated examples.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.434
They show a pattern:

00:11:41.434 --> 00:11:47.330
decades of cover-up and conspiracy
with John Swinney at the heart.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:51.990
No failure is too serious for
this Government to cover up.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:12:00.070
Is it not time John Swinney came out of
the shadows and put country before party?

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:08.487
The First Minister:

00:12:08.487 --> 00:12:14.670
I clearly have — I won my own mandate
from Scotland's voters on 7 May.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:25.430
With respect to Jackie Baillie:

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:30.335
we have worked side by side for 27 years —

00:12:30.335 --> 00:12:35.730
only the two of us are
left from that first day.

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:45.930
So she has watched my career
in government for longer

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:50.330
than I had to watch hers, if I may say so.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:13:03.000
Jackie Baillie spent 12 months in
government; I have spent 19 years.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.000
Let the public judge that.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:10.380
My final point is this:

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.780
everything Jackie Baillie just said,

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:22.220
Anas Sarwar said to me week
after week before the election —

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:26.840
and Labour fell to 17 seats.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:27.400
[Interruption.]

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:33.180
Mr Bibby shouts at me, having learned
nothing from the election defeat

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:37.400
— that all that stuff does not work.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:45.900
The public can see Labour for what it
is: no ideas, no hope, no direction —

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.700
and that will not change this weekend.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:58.524
Jackie Baillie: He cannot escape
it: everything I said is true.

00:13:58.524 --> 00:14:01.960
Much as I have enjoyed our 27 years,

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:08.360
I suspect that, if Stephen Flynn has his
way, I will outlast the First Minister —

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.960
[Interruption.]

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:18.240
The SNP would always rather
hide a problem than fix it —

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.470
[Interruption.]

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:25.370
This next matter is very serious.
I hope SNP members will listen.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:26.576
The Presiding Officer:
Let us hear the question.

00:14:26.576 --> 00:14:30.576
Jackie Baillie: The SNP hides
problems instead of fixing them.

00:14:30.576 --> 00:14:32.176
That has real consequences.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:34.410
Here is one example.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:38.010
John Swinney has promised
for years to fix ambulances.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:43.280
We challenged him on the
ambulance crisis in May,

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:48.170
September and November last
year, and again in January.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:55.118
He stood right there and promised to
fix the Scottish Ambulance Service.

00:14:55.118 --> 00:14:56.770
He never does.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:03.257
This summer, Julie Parker lay dying on
her bathroom floor, waiting for help.

00:15:03.257 --> 00:15:05.910
Police found her and phoned 999.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:09.130
They phoned again when no ambulance came.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:13.770
An ambulance took four hours.
By then it was too late.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:16.710
She was just 55 years old.

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:20.410
That is the human cost of SNP failure.

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:25.225
When will John Swinney
realise that dishonesty

00:15:25.225 --> 00:15:28.150
and broken promises have consequences?

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:37.367
The First Minister: Jackie
Baillie uses very strong language.

00:15:37.367 --> 00:15:42.830
I will deal with the case seriously,
as a First Minister should.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:50.730
But first, on freedom of
information and transparency:

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.715
in the past six months, the Government

00:15:55.715 --> 00:15:58.410
received 3,218 freedom
of information requests

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:03.930
— 50 per cent more than five years ago.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:09.110
We answered 96 per cent of them
within the legal time limit.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:14.430
So, with evidence, I reject
Jackie Baillie's claim

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:19.750
that the Government is not
meeting its obligations.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:27.530
Now to the very serious matter of the
human cost in Julie Parker's case.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:29.668
I know the case.

00:16:29.668 --> 00:16:35.770
The Health Secretary has offered to meet
Julie Parker's family after a review

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:39.650
by the Scottish Ambulance Service
and NHS Ayrshire and Arran.

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:44.313
Cases like Julie Parker's,
and Elizabeth Crammond's,

00:16:44.313 --> 00:16:48.310
which Jackie Baillie raised last week,

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:56.430
show unacceptable health service
performance. I do not hide from that.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:59.412
We work hard to improve outcomes,

00:16:59.412 --> 00:17:04.110
but sadly some cases will
not go as we would want,

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:07.310
and we must learn the lessons and improve.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.150
That drives the Government's agenda.

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:15.370
We try to deliver the
improvements that are needed,

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:21.310
and the Health Secretary will discuss
the case with Julie Parker's family.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:25.310
I close by sending my condolences
to Julie Parker's family.

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:33.850
This morning, in the Daily Record,
I read the transcript of the call

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:39.490
between the police and the Scottish
Ambulance Service. It is deeply disturbing

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:43.490
and must be examined. That
is exactly what will happen.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:48.470
Ross Greer: The Greens send condolences
to Cathie Craigie's family and friends.

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:53.590
Her landlord register
began an important journey

00:17:53.590 --> 00:18:00.110
to protect renters, which Parliament is
still on. It has made a real difference.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:04.645
Congratulations to the
community groups whose campaign

00:18:04.645 --> 00:18:09.410
against huge data centres has
forced the Government to act.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.630
But the Government's
position is still confused.

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:17.490
Ministers say they will give
councils new guidance by Christmas.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.430
That is welcome — the
Greens called for it.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.890
But ministers also say no
decisions before then.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:26.530
That does not match the real timeline.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:31.810
Larbert, Greenock and Auchtertool
could all be decided before Christmas.

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.946
Larbert's deadline is 4 October,

00:18:34.946 --> 00:18:40.050
and Greenock's developers are
working to a November deadline.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:46.610
The Government says clearly: no decisions
before the guidance at Christmas.

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:49.181
So has it told Inverclyde,

00:18:49.181 --> 00:18:53.810
Falkirk and Fife councils to
pause those live applications?

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.960
The First Minister: We do not
expect councils to decide any

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:05.960
applications before our planning
guidance is issued later this year.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:14.585
Ross Greer: What ministers said yesterday
does not match what they voted for,

00:19:14.585 --> 00:19:17.700
or what the First Minister just said.

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.540
Last night, communities
across Scotland celebrated

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:27.140
because they thought there was a formal
pause. We take that in good faith.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:30.700
But MSPs left the chamber confused:

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:34.480
ministers' words did
not match their votes.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.880
The Business Minister said no decisions
are expected before Christmas,

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.080
after talks with council officials

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:42.280
— as the First Minister just said.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.820
But nothing public confirms that.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:47.697
Today, Falkirk Council's website still

00:19:47.697 --> 00:19:52.300
gives 4 October as the
deadline for Larbert.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:20:01.340
If a council decides a data centre before
Christmas — as some still say they will —

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.743
will the Government step in and take the

00:20:04.743 --> 00:20:07.720
final decision itself,
using its new guidance?

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:14.667
The First Minister: As I said, we
do not expect any applications to

00:20:14.667 --> 00:20:19.226
be decided before the planning
guidance is issued later this year.

00:20:19.226 --> 00:20:23.370
Since the summer we have
required councils to notify us,

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:27.100
so we watch live applications
and their timescales closely

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.500
— hence what I just told Parliament.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:36.162
Scottish ministers have call-in
powers, but we use them sparingly,

00:20:36.162 --> 00:20:39.560
respecting councils'
role in local decisions.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:46.680
But we can call in any application when it
is necessary and of national importance.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.729
Ministers can also take over a planning

00:20:50.729 --> 00:20:55.060
appeal instead of leaving
it to a reporter —

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:58.440
as we did recently for a
data centre in Edinburgh.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:02.440
I hope that gives Ross
Greer the clarity he wants.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.140
Ross Greer: That answer
does not give clarity.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:11.600
It hints the Government would
step in, and I welcome that.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:17.760
But Falkirk Council still says
its deadline is 4 October.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:20.952
The Government's private
talks with councils

00:21:20.952 --> 00:21:24.280
seem very different
from the public record.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:28.280
That must be fixed for
communities to trust the process.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:33.019
President Connolly of Ireland
is in Scotland this week.

00:21:33.019 --> 00:21:36.300
She leads on solidarity with Palestine —

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.500
but Ireland also shows what happens when

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:42.700
greedy tech companies
build whatever they want.

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:47.980
A third of Ireland's energy
goes to data centres —

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:50.920
and Scotland's plans are far bigger.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:54.100
The first 24 applications
here would need one

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:57.280
and a half times Scotland's
entire energy demand.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:00.140
This week Parliament was warned
it could push up energy bills.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.020
That is exactly what happened in Ireland.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:09.800
Data centres added £300 a
year to Irish energy bills.

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:14.720
Councils cannot consider that when
deciding applications one by one.

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:18.220
So, will the Government's guidance

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:24.040
include a clear national
strategy on how much electricity

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:29.240
data centres may use before they
harm our climate and push up bills?

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:37.270
The First Minister: The
national guidance will set out

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:40.770
everything councils must consider
when deciding applications.

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:46.310
To repeat: since the summer, we have
required councils to notify us,

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:51.770
so we are watching live applications
and their timescales closely.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:56.030
Mr Greer raised the
role of tech companies.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:06.230
In its amendment yesterday, the Government
insisted tech companies must be regulated.

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:10.359
That was central to our
amendment to the Green motion,

00:23:10.359 --> 00:23:13.250
and I am pleased it was passed,

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:18.210
because it strengthens
the case my Government can

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:22.730
make to the UK Government
for proper regulation,

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:28.350
given the concerns raised over the
weekend, especially about tech companies.

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:34.242
I was surprised the Greens
abstained on that vote,

00:23:34.242 --> 00:23:36.630
because regulation is necessary.

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:42.751
It is central to my Cabinet's concerns
about violence against women and girls,

00:23:42.751 --> 00:23:46.370
because the online world
makes regulation more urgent.

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:52.501
As we tackle these questions
there are big issues to face,

00:23:52.501 --> 00:23:56.470
and I hope the Greens will work with us.

00:23:56.470 --> 00:24:01.530
Mr Greer mentioned President Connolly.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:06.104
It was a real pleasure
for my wife and me to

00:24:06.104 --> 00:24:09.430
welcome her and her husband
to Scotland yesterday.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:14.630
President Connolly is an
outstanding world leader.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:19.970
She is a voice for peace,
dialogue and respect.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:27.110
She has much to say to Scotland,
and Scotland is listening carefully.

00:24:30.950 --> 00:24:34.800
Russell Findlay: I, too,
send my condolences,

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:38.650
and my party's, to
Cathie Craigie's family.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:44.677
John Swinney's new friends in Sinn Féin
have never condemned IRA terrorism.

00:24:44.677 --> 00:24:50.353
They still justify the
killing of innocent men,

00:24:50.353 --> 00:24:54.610
women and children,
including Scottish soldiers.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:25:00.650
Mr Swinney shamefully told IRA victims
and their grieving families to "move on".

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:06.310
Yet the same politician has never

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:11.890
accepted the clear result of
Scotland's independence referendum,

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:17.090
and he is now working with Sinn
Féin to break up our country.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:23.924
Why should those hurt or
bereaved by the IRA move on,

00:25:23.924 --> 00:25:27.790
when the SNP refuses to move on?

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:32.701
The First Minister:

00:25:32.701 --> 00:25:37.470
I have always expressed sympathy
for the victims of terrorism.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:43.146
Just last month, I represented
Scotland at the first

00:25:43.146 --> 00:25:47.570
national day of remembrance
for victims of terrorism,

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:51.310
held by the Prime Minister in London.

00:25:52.230 --> 00:26:01.210
There I met victims' families, many
affected by the Lockerbie bombing in 1988.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:04.885
I condemn terrorism and
every terrorist act,

00:26:04.885 --> 00:26:07.590
without reservation, and I always have.

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:15.390
I also accepted the result of the
2014 referendum with good grace.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:20.790
[Interruption.]

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:31.070
After that referendum, I took part in
good faith in the Smith commission,

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:37.550
which reviewed Parliament's powers
and led to a big increase in them.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:44.257
I argued in that process
for this Parliament

00:26:44.257 --> 00:26:47.150
to have more impact on people's lives.

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:53.727
I did that in good faith, to
strengthen Parliament within the UK.

00:26:53.727 --> 00:26:56.910
Mr Findlay's point is pointless, as usual.

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:01.890
People would be surprised if I abandoned

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:05.190
the principle that
brought me into politics.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:10.110
I entered politics
because I became convinced

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:14.637
that the people best placed
to decide Scotland's

00:27:14.637 --> 00:27:19.730
future are the people
who choose to live here.

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:25.330
So we need a referendum on Scotland's
future. I will deliver it this term.

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:34.940
Russell Findlay: John Swinney
has condemned terrorism —

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:39.740
but has he asked his Sinn
Féin friends to do the same?

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:45.940
We just heard again that John
Swinney cares about one thing only:

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:51.640
making Scotland poorer and
weaker by leaving the UK.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:57.360
He and his party have never
respected the referendum result,

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:03.058
and Scotland pays a heavy
price for the SNP's obsession:

00:28:03.058 --> 00:28:07.858
a broken NHS, failing schools, victims
let down by the justice system,

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:12.780
record high taxes, and
out-of-control benefit spending.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:19.340
People want the Government to focus
on the issues that matter to them

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:24.980
— not independence.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:28.910
So why does his constitutional
obsession matter

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:32.840
more than fixing Scotland's
broken public services?

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:37.174
The First Minister: Interesting —

00:28:37.174 --> 00:28:42.880
today Russell Findlay has asked the
same questions as Malcolm Offord.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:47.736
That shows the merger is under way between

00:28:47.736 --> 00:28:53.200
those two forces on that
side of the chamber.

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:59.660
My goodness, it will be some
marriage of convenience

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:04.680
between the Conservatives and
Reform, given what they all say.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:07.600
That will be a sight worth seeing.

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:10.389
They have a lot in common:

00:29:10.389 --> 00:29:14.107
Malcolm Offord was a
member of the Government

00:29:14.107 --> 00:29:17.360
that imposed austerity on this country —

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:17.613
[Interruption.]

00:29:17.613 --> 00:29:17.992
Stephen Kerr: Answer the question.

00:29:17.992 --> 00:29:21.400
The Presiding Officer: Mr Kerr,
let us hear the First Minister.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:27.250
The First Minister: Malcolm Offord was a
member, and Russell Findlay a supporter,

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:31.300
of a Government that imposed
austerity on this country.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:36.124
They both come from political
traditions that wanted Brexit,

00:29:36.124 --> 00:29:39.340
which has clearly made us poorer.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:45.560
So I will take no lessons from Russell
Findlay or his pal Malcolm Offord —

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:45.800
Stephen Kerr: Answer the question!

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:45.880
The Presiding Officer:
Excuse me, First Minister.

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:49.580
Mr Kerr, as I have said, please
stop shouting from the back.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:52.460
You can give your view without yelling.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:53.880
We are trying to hear the First Minister.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:56.480
If I cannot hear him, how can anyone else?

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:57.600
First Minister, please finish.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:01.580
The First Minister: I will take
no lessons from the new pals

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:08.720
Russell Findlay and Malcolm Offord — who
gave us decline, austerity and Brexit.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.520
Instead, I will inspire people with a

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.920
positive vision of
independence for Scotland.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:22.825
Alex Cole-Hamilton: Like others,

00:30:22.825 --> 00:30:28.240
I send the Scottish Liberal Democrats'
heartfelt condolences to the Labour Party,

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:31.420
to Cathie Craigie's family
and everyone who loved her.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:35.420
She was a towering figure
and will be much missed.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:40.600
This week, Lady Justice Thirlwall
published her report into the

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:45.160
horrific murders of babies at
the Countess of Chester hospital.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:53.160
The review followed the conviction of
nurse Lucy Letby. It was a shocking case.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:31:03.240
What has the First Minister's
Government learned from the inquiry?

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:07.320
The First Minister: Clearly,
we must all learn lessons from

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:11.400
the inquiry about the quality
and safety of patient care.

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:17.800
As Mr Cole-Hamilton knows, a constant
priority of the Scottish Government

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:26.040
is to strengthen patient safety in
every part of our health service.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:30.992
That was at the heart of my
answer to Jackie Baillie,

00:31:30.992 --> 00:31:34.360
because vital issues of
patient safety were involved.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:38.920
That will be our focus
as we study the report,

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:42.504
so that any lessons relevant to Scotland,

00:31:42.504 --> 00:31:46.600
especially in maternity services,
are learned and applied.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:50.900
Alex Cole-Hamilton: Good.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:56.500
But the inquiry's recommendations stop at
the border; the risk to babies does not.

00:31:56.780 --> 00:32:03.240
Parents want to know newborns here get the
same protection as babies everywhere else.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:08.660
Lady Justice Thirlwall recommends
live cameras over cots,

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:14.540
tighter controls on insulin,
and a duty on every health trust

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:19.400
to monitor all baby deaths
and act on worrying patterns.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:24.700
This Government must now decide whether
to adopt these vital protections,

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:30.380
because, God forbid, if Scotland
ever had its own Lucy Letby,

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:35.020
we must have every possible
safeguard to keep our babies safe.

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:42.520
Will the Government bring in the same
protections in Scotland — and by when?

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:49.820
The First Minister: Mr Cole-Hamilton
raises important issues,

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:55.240
and I expect them to be central
to the Government's work

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:59.323
through the maternity
and neonatal task force,

00:32:59.323 --> 00:33:05.740
set up to improve maternity and
newborn care and birth outcomes.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:12.340
We set up the task force to do that work.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:18.622
It makes sense for the
task force to look at the

00:33:18.622 --> 00:33:22.620
issues in the report Mr
Cole-Hamilton mentions,

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:27.000
and to find where Scotland's current
practice should be strengthened.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:30.960
I encourage the task
force to work in that way,

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:35.630
so that every lesson that
can be applied is applied,

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:40.300
to meet the high standards Mr
Cole-Hamilton asks for today.

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:43.840
The Presiding Officer:
Thank you, First Minister.

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:49.920
Earlier I said "Mr Kerr" — I meant Stephen
Kerr. There are five Mr Kerrs here,

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.720
and I apologise to the other four.
